1.3: the `lim <= topk` early-out is bit-exact, 22 % faster in the kernel, and worth nothing

Ladder item 1.3, closed as a MEASURED NULL rather than a win. The change is real: below ctx 2048
every candidate is already in the top-k, and `topk_radix_select` was spending a whole radix level
(256-bin clear, a strided pass with a shared atomicAdd per survivor, a serial scan on thread 0)
discovering that. Skipping it is provably identical output — the threshold it would have computed
is <= every candidate's composite, so `thr = 0` selects the same set — and standalone it is worth
+2.1 to +4.1 us per call at T <= 512, +0.00 +/- 0.03 us above it.

In situ it is 0.07 % of a forward, and that was pre-registered in the script header before the run:
21 ratio-4 layers x ~4 us = 0.085 ms of a ~130 ms step.

MEASURED, paired, OFF arm first so drift penalises the early-out, 34/34 legs byte-identical in
generated token ids, tau identical to three decimals on every leg:

  ctx 6248 control  147.45 -> 147.69  (+0.24)   <- T=1565, early CANNOT fire: identical code
  ctx 6260 sweep    144.48 -> 144.83  (+0.35)   <- likewise
  ctx 1656 control  141.17 -> 141.11  (-0.06)
  ctx 1664 sweep    135.72 -> 135.81  (+0.10)
  ctx  889 sweep    131.72 -> 131.83  (+0.11)
  ctx  492 sweep    126.86 -> 126.33  (-0.53)
  ctx  249 sweep    127.29 -> 127.08  (-0.21)

  b = 3.008 +/- 0.241 -> 3.036 +/- 0.240   (2.977 -> 3.006 width-controlled); a = 127.92 -> 127.84

The two ctx-6144 legs are the point: both arms run provably identical code there and still differed
by +0.24 and +0.35 ms, so the null is stated against a zero measured INSIDE the experiment rather
than against a 3.5 % spread quoted from another day. i:topk, the only mark that brackets the changed
launch, did see it: 0.42 -> 0.28 ms at ctx 768, 0.52 -> 0.34 at 1536, 0.72 -> 0.72 at 6144.

KEPT, default on (`DSV4_TOPK_EARLY=0` restores the full search), because it is strictly less work
for identical output. Gates: unit gate over 4 kernel shapes x 13 lengths x 6 distributions with the
early-out both ON and OFF; in-situ DSV4_TOPK_GATE memcmp, 125 PASS / 0 FAIL over 7,808 checks and
66,972,918 index slots; standing argmax GATE; LOSSLESS gate at tau 2.87.

WHAT IT MEANS, which is worth more than the item. 1.3 was ranked above 1.5 on 0.4's attribution of
i:topk at 13.47 ms at ctx 12,288. 1.2 took i:topk to 0.72 ms one iteration earlier, so this was
built against a headroom that no longer existed and nobody re-derived it. A ranked work list is a
function of a cost model and the item above it just changed the cost model. New ladder rule 6:
re-attribute before you pick — it is free, because every A/B here already runs a dprof pair.

Rule 7 is its counterweight, from the near-miss it caused. This iteration's re-attribution fits
cattn:sparse at 1.694 +/- 0.065 ms/1000 against 0.4's 0.709 +/- 0.050 — which taken at face value
promotes item 1.7 above 1.5. It is one concave curve read over two windows: per-point medians run
9.35/15.72/19.89 ms at ctx 768/1536/6144 and 19.22/19.90/21.17 at 3072/6144/12,288, i.e. 8.29 ms per
1000 across the first leg and 0.208 across the last, which is a top-k gather saturating once context
exceeds k x ratio. i:score over the same samples is 0.88/1.25/3.53 = 0.482 then 0.495 per 1000,
genuinely linear. The ladder order stands and 1.5 is still the next kernel.

Wiki, same commit: negative-results.md §4c (adopted, bit-exact, worth nothing — and why it was
built), measurement-and-traps.md §15 (put a leg the change cannot touch inside the same sweep) and
§16 (a fitted slope is only ms/1000 if the mark is linear), context-scaling.md (1.3 status and the
post-1.2 re-attribution table), kernel-optimisations.md §2.6 (cross-reference), README.md state
table — where 1.3 appears as a blank on purpose.

Evidence: evidence/decode_loop/{fit_1p3_paired,fit_1p3_on,fit_1p3_off,fit_1p3_itopk}.txt,
gate_topk_radix_1p3.log, server_1p3_gate.log, dprof_ctx_1p3_{on,off}.txt,
decode_1p3_lossless.log, topk_early_ab.log.
